OPC UA PubSub Latency Test (UDP + UADP)
✅ Goal

This test aims to evaluate how well a Windows system handles periodic communication using the OPC UA PubSub model over UDP multicast, with UADP encoding.
The main objective is to determine whether Windows can sustain low-latency transmission at a fixed rate (e.g., 100 ms), and whether it is suitable for near real-time tasks.

🧪 Test Setup

1 Publisher (udp_pub_test.c)
1 Subscriber (udp_latency_test.c)

Both running on the same machine
Communication is one-way only (Pub → Sub)
Messages are sent over multicast UDP

🔁 Flow Diagram

+----------------+                        +----------------+
|                |   Multicast UDP (UADP) |                |
|   Publisher    |----------------------->|   Subscriber   |
| (udp_pub_test) |   232.1.2.100:4840     | (udp_latency)  |
|                |                        |                |
+----------------+                        +----------------+
📦 Data Structure

Each published UADP message contains 5 fields encoded as Variants:

Field #	    Type	    Description
0	        UInt64	    Timestamp in microseconds (since boot)
1	        Byte	    Dummy value
2	        UInt16	    Dummy value
3	        DateTime	Current UTC time
4	        UInt32	    Dummy value

The subscriber only uses Field 0 to calculate the latency:
latency = current_time_us - received_timestamp


🕓 Timing Parameters

Parameter	            Value
Publish Interval	    Every 100 ms
Timestamp Precision	    Microseconds
Network Protocol        UDP Multicast
Multicast Address	    232.1.2.100
Port	                4840
Interface (Rx)	        10.51.140.223
UADP Message Type	    KeyFrame
Sequence Numbers	    Enabled

📈 Output Example

Latency: 0 us
Latency: 0 us
Latency: 16 us
Latency: 0 us

CSV Log (latency_log.csv)

timestamp,latency_us
2778765,0
2778875,0
2779750,16
2781343,0
...
This allows for post-analysis of timing and jitter.

🧠 What This Test Tells You
-Verifies that the Publisher sends at a fixed interval (100 ms)
-Measures the time between Publisher timestamp and Subscriber reception
-Detects latency anomalies (spikes, jitter)
-Helps validate deterministic timing behavior on Windows

⚠️ Limitations
-Not a network latency test (no NIC-to-NIC path)
-No hardware timestamping (since both apps run on same OS)
-Windows is not a real-time OS – jitter may happen

Wireshark is not reliable in this setup because TX and RX occur locally











